## 6. Conclusion: A Two-Layer Model of Japanese Income Tax 
LawLAYER 1 — Core Categories (Substantively Governed by the Source Theory)
Categories: Interest, Dividends, Real Estate, Business, Employment,
Retirement, Timber, Capital Gains
Criteria: Is there continuity/recurrence? Is it consideration (quid pro quo)?
Scope: Ordinary market transactions and contract-like illegal enterprises
(e.g., usurious interest, illegal bookmaking businesses)


If criteria are NOT met (Unilateral Expropriations / Residual Gains)

LAYER 2 — Gap-Filling Categories (Governed by the Comprehensive Income Concept)
Categories: Occasional Income, Miscellaneous Income,
and structural rules separating production costs from consumption
Criteria: Is there immediate faculty/ability to pay (no probability of restitution)?
Has spendable economic capacity been realized?
Scope: (1) Captures non-recurring windfalls and unilateral crimes
(e.g., larceny, robbery, bribes, one-off embezzlement)
(2) Denies deduction for personal living expenses

Article 34(1) demonstrates that the source theory was never abandoned by the Japanese legislature. It remains the governing doctrinal test for the eight core categories of income. The comprehensive income concept performs a vital supporting role: functioning as a statutory safety net for extraordinary receipts and policing the boundary between deductible business expenses and non-deductible consumption.
